    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. But some critics might say managed conservation is actually more practical because we have to deal with existing human infrastructure and climate change, which rewilding might ignore. Can you add a section that addresses those practicalities while still making it clear why rewilding is the superior long-term strategy?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While acknowledging that climate change and existing infrastructure present significant practical hurdles, relying solely on managed conservation merely treats symptoms rather than addressing root causes. Rewilding approaches are not laissez-faire; they strategically address human interfaces by fostering resilient, diverse ecosystems capable of absorbing climate shocks more effectively than managed monocultures. The long-term viability of conservation demands that we move from handicapping nature to enabling its fundamental resilience against unavoidable pressures. Thus, rewilding offers a robust framework for adaptation that managed, fixed interventions cannot match.",
      "source": "target",
